[[17592186092793 :question/problem "If $k(1 - (\\frac{1}{3})^{10})$ is the sum of the first $10$ terms of the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{4}{9} + \\frac{4}{27} + ⋯$, then $k =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186092793 :question/answer-fields 17592186090928] [17592186064026 :question/problem "A cube is shrinking uniformly at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm}^3/\\text{s}$. How fast is each side of the cube shrinking when the volume of the cube is $27\\,\\text{mm}^3$?\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7021995b1a501f6cb862b33649bdb1927f7ce4a8630a9e261382a193f40a4.png)"] [17592186064026 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and $V$ be its volume. We are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{dV}{dt} =-3mm^{3}/s\n$$\n\nWe would like to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nV=27\\,\\text{mm}^3\n$$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n$V=x^3$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}V &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 3x^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when\n\n$$\nV = 27\\,\\text{mm}^3\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= x^{3} \\\\\n27 &= x^{3} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm dt}=-3\n$$\n\ninto the expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 3x^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n- 3 &= 3(3)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -\\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are shrinking at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nat the moment when\n\n$$\nx=3\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$"] [17592186064027 :question/problem "All of the edges of a cube are increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$. How fast is the surface area of the cube changing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\text{cm}^3$?\n\n![](images/a6/a6ef2af1ed8e44f82469625e232ed67c0b809a3c8199a6ce52fd2bf2e3fcffa7.png)"] [17592186064027 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2mm/s\n$$\n\nWe would like to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d S}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nV= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$. So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS= 6x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}S &= \\frac{d}{dt}(6x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 12x\\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when\n\n$$\nV= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= x^{3} \\\\\n8 &= x^{3} \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nx=2\\,\\text{cm}=20\\,\\text{mm}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 12x\\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 12(20)(2) \\\\\n\\frac{dS}{dt} &= 480\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of $480 \\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{s}$."] [17592186064028 :question/problem "Water is being poured into a rectangular prism-shaped tank. The length and width of this tank are $20 \\textrm{ft}$ and $15 \\textrm{ft}$, respectively. If the height of the water increases at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{ft/min}$, determine the rate at which the volume of stored water increases.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7c6749ec0585ab714640fa9155eba212e2a9c628b50cc3c3de07492b8b3aec.png)"] [17592186064028 :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=20\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad W=15\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h$, is given by the formula for the volume of the water in the tank:\n$V = LWh$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$, treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(V) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(LWh) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= LW\\frac{dh}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= (20)(15)\\frac{dh}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 300 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is rising at a rate of $50\\,\\text{ft}^3\\textrm{/min}$."] [17592186064732 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2be88ec9cb7559ca8d80d528e143d4f2dd72109c9dc576b353dd352855c53ff.png)\n\nA cube is expanding uniformly at a rate of $57cm^{3}/s$. How fast is each edge of the cube increasing when the volume of the cube is $27cm^{3}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186064732 :question/answer-fields 17592186062419] [17592186092017 :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{N}a_1 r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term of the series, $r$ is the common ratio, and $N$ is the number of terms in the series. In this case, $a_1 =4$ and $N = 5$.\n\nTo find $r$, we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{8}{4} = 2\n$$\n\nNow, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n4+8+16+32+64 = \\sum_{n=1}^{5}4 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above series using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(5)4 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} &= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)4 \\cdot 2^{n} \\cdot 2^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)4 \\cdot 2^{n} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{n} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)2 \\cdot 2^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092018 :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{N}a_1 r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term of the series, $r$ is the common ratio, and $N$ is the number of terms in the series. In this case, $a_1 = 1$ and $N = 5$.\n\nTo find $r$, we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{-4}{1} = -4\n$$\n\nSo, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n1 - 4 + 16 - 64 + 256 = \\sum_{n=1}^{5} 1 \\cdot (-4)^{n - 1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 4 + 16 - 64 + 256 &= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)1 \\cdot (-4)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)(-4)^{n}(-4)^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)(-4)^{n}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)(-\\frac{1}{4})(-4)^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092019 :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{N}a_1 r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, $a_1 = 1$.\n\nTo find $r$, we find the ratio of the consecutive terms $a_1$ and $a_2$:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{3}{1} = 3\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\na_N = a_1 \\cdot r^{N - 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{N} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{N - 1} \\\\\n243 &= 1 \\cdot 3^{N - 1} \\\\\n3^{5} &= 3^{N - 1} \\\\\n5 &= N - 1 \\\\\nN &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 3 + 9 + ⋯ + 243 &= ∑_(n = 1)^(6)1 \\cdot 3^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(6)3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092020 :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{N}a_1 r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term of the series, $r$ is the common ratio, and $N$ is the number of terms in the series. In this case, $a_1 = 4$ and $N = 5$.\n\nTo find $r$, we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{12}{4} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 + 12 + 36 + 108 + 324 &= ∑_(n = 1)^(5)4 \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198021 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y$,\nwhere $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}$."] [17592186198021 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D$,\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 1 \\le v \\le 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{y - x}{x + y}dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{u}{v}\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ)\\frac{u}{v} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{4}[\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}du]dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[\\int_{0}^{1}udu]dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{1}dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}dv \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{4}\\ln \\mid v \\mid \\right|_{1}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\ln 4 - \\ln 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198022 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (2x+y)y^2 \\:\\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y$,\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $\\left(\\dfrac32,0\\right)$, $\\left(\\dfrac52,0\\right)$, $(1,3)$, and $(0,3)$."] [17592186198022 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y, \\qquad v=y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2}, 0) &= (2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} + 0, 0) \\\\\n&= (3, 0) \\\\\nT^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}, 0) &= (2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} + 0, 0) \\\\\n&= (5, 0) \\\\\nT^{-1}(1, 3) &= (2 \\cdot 1 + 3, 3) \\\\\n&= (5, 3) \\\\\nT^{-1}(0, 3) &= (2 \\cdot 0 + 3, 3) \\\\\n&= (3, 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(2x + y)y^{2}dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)uv^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ)uv^{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{3}^{5}uv^{2}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{3}v^{2}dv\\int_{3}^{5}udu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}v^{3}]_{0}^{3} \\cdot [\\frac{1}{2}u^{2}]_{3}^{5}dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198241 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}$.\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x$, write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v$.\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y$"] [17592186198241 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v): - 2 \\le u \\le 2, 0 \\le v \\le 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6\n$$\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\sin (3(x + y))dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)\\sin (3(\\frac{u - 2v}{6} + \\frac{u + 4v}{6}))\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ)\\sin (3(\\frac{2u + 2v}{6})) & \\frac{1}{6}\\end{vmatrix}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\sin(u + v)dudv\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198585 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$∬_(D)((x + y)^{2})/(2x + y)dxdy$,\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(0, 1)$, $(1, 0)$, $(-1, 3)$, and $(0, 2)$."] [17592186198585 :question/answer-fields 17592186194111] [17592186198957 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n$∬_(D)(1)/(1 + (x - y)^{2})dxdy$,\nwhere $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le x - y \\le 2}$."] [17592186198957 :question/answer-fields 17592186197582] [17592186152015 :question/problem "If $\\overline{X} = \\{a, c, d\\}$ and $\\overline{Y} = \\{a, e, i, o, u \\}$, then what is $\\overline{X \\cup Y}$?"] [17592186152015 :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law for unions states that\n$\\overline{X \\cup Y} \\equiv \\overline{X} \\cap \\overline{Y}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX∪Y^(―) &= X^(―)∩Y^(―) \\\\\n&= {a, c, d}∩{a, e, i, o, u} \\\\\n&= {a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152016 :question/problem "If $\\overline{P} = \\{2, 4, 6 \\}$ and $\\overline{Q} = \\{1, 2, 4 \\}$, then what is $\\overline{P \\cap Q}$?"] [17592186152016 :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law for intersections states that\n$\\overline{P \\cap Q} \\equiv \\overline{P} \\cup \\overline{Q}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP∩Q^(―) &= P^(―)∪Q^(―) \\\\\n&= {2, 4, 6}∪{1, 2, 4} \\\\\n&= {1, 2, 4, 6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092913 :question/problem "What is $\\sinh (-3)$?"] [17592186092913 :question/answer-fields 17592186091648] [17592186188000 :question/problem "Since the year $2000$, a herd of antelopes multiplies at a rate proportional to their population. Also, the number of antelopes in the herd decreases by $20$ antelopes per year due to predators. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the population of antelopes at time $t$, where $t$ is the time (in years) since the year $2000$?"] [17592186188000 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the number of antelopes per year increases in proportion to $P$ and decreases by $20$. The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t}=kP - 20\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality."] [17592186188001 :question/problem "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate inversely proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t)$, in this process."] [17592186188001 :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ *increases* at a rate *inversely proportional to its radius.* Therefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{k}{r}$,\nwhere $k > 0$.\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{4}{3}πr^{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 4πr^{2}\\frac{dr}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{r} \\\\\n4πr^{2}\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{r} \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= ((\\frac{k}{r}))/(4πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{4πr^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{4\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{K}{r^3}\n$$"] [17592186188002 :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative $y'(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-1,2)$ equals $2$. What differential equation describes this curve?"] [17592186188002 :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** $y'(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance from $P$ to the origin.***\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $x^2+y^2$.\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional to* the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have\n$y'(x) = k\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}$\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\ny'(x) = \\dfrac{k}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $y'(x) = 2$ when at the point $(-1, 2)$. We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= (k)/((-1)^{2} + 2^{2}) \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{k}{5} \\\\\nk &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\ny'(x)= \\dfrac{10}{x^2+y^2}\n$$"] [17592186188209 :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t)$, in this process."] [17592186188209 :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to its *radius.* In other words,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kr$\nwhere $k > 0$.\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by\n$A=\\pi r^2$.\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= 2πr\\frac{dr}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= -kr \\\\\n2πr\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -kr \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{kr}{2πr} \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{k}{2π}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{2\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = -K\n$$"] [17592186083000 :question/problem "Evaluate the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{8} 3 (2)^{n-1}$."] [17592186083000 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N a_1 r^{n-1}}\n$$\n\nit is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1 = 3$, $r = 2$, and $N = 8$. If we substitute these values into the formula for $S_N$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(8)3(2)^{n - 1} &= 3 \\cdot (\\frac{1 - 2^{8}}{1 - 2}) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (\\frac{-255}{-1}) \\\\\n&= 765\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083001 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{5} 3 (4)^{n}$."] [17592186083001 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N a r^{n}}\n$$\n\nit is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r = 4$ and $N = 5$. We need to figure out the value of $a_1$.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\na_n = 3(4)^n\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term $a_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 3(4)^{1} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $a_1 = 12$, $r = 4$, and $N = 5$ into the formula for $S_N$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(5)3(4)^{n} &= 12 \\cdot ((1 - 4^{5}))/(1 - 4) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{12}{3}(-1023) \\\\\n&= 4092\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083002 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=0}^{4} 3\\left(2\\right)^{n-1}$."] [17592186083002 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum{a_1 r^{n-1}}$, it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1$.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_0\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nNote that we use $a_0$ in the sum formula because $a_0$ is the first term of the series.\n\nIn this case, $r =2$. Also, note that $N=5$, since there are $5$ terms in total: $a_0$, $a_1$, $a_2$, $a_3$, $a_4$.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\na_n = 3\\left(2\\right)^{n-1}\n$$\n\nso the first term $a_0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{0} &= 3(2)^{0 - 1} \\\\\n&= 3(2)^{-1} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute\n\n$$\na_0 =\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\n$r=2$, and $N=5$ into the formula for $S_N$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{5} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot ((1 - 2^{5}))/((1 - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot ((-31))/((-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{93}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083003 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n = 1}^{5} \\dfrac{8}{2^{n-1}}\\,$."] [17592186083003 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^{5} \\dfrac{8}{2^{n-1}} = \\sum_{n = 1}^{5} 8 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N a_1 r^{n-1}}\n$$\n\nit is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum $S_N$ of a geometric series:\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1 =8$,\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand $N = 5$. If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(5)\\frac{8}{2^{n - 1}} &= 8 \\cdot ([1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{5}])/((1 - \\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot ((\\frac{31}{32}))/((\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot \\frac{31}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{31}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083908 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $∑_(n = 1)^(3)\\frac{1}{5^{n - 1}}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186083908 :question/answer-fields 17592186082349] [17592186083911 :question/problem "Determine the value of the geometric series $∑_(n = 0)^(7)8(\\frac{3}{5})^{n - 1}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186083911 :question/answer-fields 17592186082367] [17592186134144 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨z^{2}, 2y, x^{2} + y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S$."] [17592186134144 :question/answer-fields 17592186130990] [17592186125164 :question/problem "Consider the set of matrices ${A, B}$, where\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{x_{1}}{x_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_{1}A + x_{2}B = O$, where $O$ is the $2 \\cdot 2$ zero-matrix."] [17592186125164 :question/answer-fields 17592186123554] [17592186147790 :question/problem "Given that for $s > ω$,\n$L{\\sinh (ωt)} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} - ω^{2}}, L{\\cosh (ωt)} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} - ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2\\cosh (6t) + \\sinh (6t)$ for $s > 6$."] [17592186147790 :question/answer-fields 17592186144444] [17592186148145 :question/problem "Given that for $s > 0$,\n$L{\\sin ωt} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, L{\\cos ωt} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 8\\cos 3t - 5\\sin 3t$ for $s > 0$."] [17592186148145 :question/answer-fields 17592186146594] [17592186151823 :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n$A = \\{2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\{2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n2. $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n3. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint"] [17592186151823 :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n$A|= {2n∣n ∈ N}; = {2(1), 2(2), 2(3), …}; = {2, 4, 6, …}$\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n$B|= {2^{-n}∣n ∈ N}; = {2^{-1}, 2^{-2}, 2^{-3}, …}; = {\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{8}, …}$.\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is\n$A \\cap B = \\{2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""] [17592186151824 :question/problem "Find the disjoint union of the sets $\\{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\}$ and $\\{v, w \\}$."] [17592186151824 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{v, w \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}}⊔{v, w} &= {-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}}∪{v, w} \\\\\n&= {-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}, v, w}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151825 :question/problem "Find the disjoint union of the sets $A_1 = \\{7,8 \\}$ and $A_2 = \\{2,4,8 \\}$."] [17592186151825 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{7,8 \\} \\cap \\{2,4,8 \\} = \\{8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}A_{1} & → & A_{1}^{∗} = {(7, 1), (8, 1)} \\\\ A_{2} & → & A_{2}^{∗} = {(2, 2), (4, 2), (8, 2)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1}⊔A_{2} &= {7, 8}⊔{2, 4, 8} \\\\\n&= A_{1}^{∗}∪A_{2}^{∗} \\\\\n&= {(7, 1), (8, 1)}∪{(2, 2), (4, 2), (8, 2)} \\\\\n&= {(7, 1), (8, 1), (2, 2), (4, 2), (8, 2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152468 :question/problem "Find the disjoint union of the sets ${a, b, c}$ and ${1, 2}$."] [17592186152468 :question/answer-fields 17592186149309] [17592186198045 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in Cartesian $xyz$-space, given by\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\:1 \\leq xy \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z\\leq 1 \\big\\}$.\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$u=xy, \\qquad v=y, \\qquad w=2z$,\n\nwrite down the triple integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 3xy^2+6yz \\, \\mathrm{d}V$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$."] [17592186198045 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right| \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy, \\qquad v=y, \\qquad w=2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v, w): 1 \\le u \\le 2, 1 \\le v \\le 2, 0 \\le w \\le 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(y - 0) \\\\\n&= 2y \\\\\n&= 2v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)3xy^{2} + 6yzdV &= ∭_(Δ)(3uv + 3vw) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) & dudvdw \\\\ = ∭_(Δ)3v(u + w) \\cdot & \\frac{1}{2v}\\end{vmatrix}dudvdw \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}(u + w)dwdvdu\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198046 :question/problem "Use the change of variables\n$u = x+y^2, \\qquad v= y+z, \\qquad w = 2z$\nto evaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_R (x+y^2)^{15}z \\: \\mathrm{d}V$,\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x+y^2 \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y+z \\leq 4, \\: 0\\leq z\\leq 2 \\big\\}$."] [17592186198046 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right| \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad v= y+z, \\qquad w = 2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v, w): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 4, 0 \\le w \\le 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)(x + y^{2})^{15}zdV &= ∭_(Δ)u^{15} \\cdot \\frac{w}{2} \\cdot \\mid \\frac{1}{2} \\mid dudvdw \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}∭_(Δ)u^{15}wdudvdw \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{4}u^{15}wdwdvdu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{0}^{1}u^{15}du \\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}dv \\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}wdw \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot [\\frac{u^{16}}{16}]_{u = 0}^{u = 1} \\cdot [v]_{v = 0}^{v = 4} \\cdot [\\frac{w^{2}}{2}]_{w = 0}^{w = 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{16} - 0) \\cdot (4 - 0) \\cdot (8 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198047 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R$, given by\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\:-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8 \\big\\}$."] [17592186198047 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as\n\n$$\nV = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right| \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R$,\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\qquad -2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\qquad 1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y, \\qquad v = y-2x, \\qquad w = z^3\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v, w): 0 \\le u \\le 1,-2 \\le v \\le 2, 1 \\le w \\le 8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3z^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3z^{2}(-1)^{3 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 9z^{2} \\\\\n&= 9w^{2/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac 19 w^{-2/3}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)dV &= ∭_(Δ)1 \\cdot \\mid \\frac{1}{9}w^{-2/3} \\mid dudvdw \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{9}w^{-2/3}dwdvdu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\\int_{0}^{1}du \\cdot \\int_{-2}^{2}dv \\cdot \\int_{1}^{8}w^{-2/3}dw \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}[u]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [v]_{-2}^{2} \\cdot [3w^{1/3}]_{1}^{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot (1 - 0) \\cdot (2 - (-2)) \\cdot 3(8^{1/3} - 1^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198630 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R$, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 1,-1 \\le 2y + z \\le 1, 1 \\le y - 2z \\le 4}$."] [17592186198630 :question/answer-fields 17592186194381] [17592186114967 :question/problem "A vat initially holds $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of a sugar solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar.\nA sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar per liter is poured into the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, while the completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. What differential equation describes the amount of sugar $Q$, in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after the inflow begins?"] [17592186114967 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t$, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$, in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that a sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0.30\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (18\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= (0.30\\frac{kg}{L}) \\cdot (18\\frac{L}{min}) \\\\\n&= 5.4\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t$.\n\n- The vat initially contains $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n- Sugar solution enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the vat.\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$. So, after $t$ minutes, $30t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the vat.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\n360 + 18t - 30t = (360-12t)\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$, *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= (30\\frac{L}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\frac{kg}{L}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{30Q}{360 - 12t}\\frac{kg}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5Q}{60 - 2t}\\frac{kg}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 5.4 - \\frac{5Q}{60 - 2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{5}{60 - 2t}Q = 5.4\n$$"] [17592186114968 :question/problem "A reactor initially holds $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of a chemical solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved reactant.\nPure solvent is pumped into the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$, while the completely mixed chemical solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent pumping begins."] [17592186114968 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t$. Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$, in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nNote that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$. So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\cdot (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\\\\n&= (0\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\cdot (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\\\\n&= 0\\frac{lb}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t$.\n\nThe reactor initially contains $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of chemical solution; solvent enters at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and drains out at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$.\nAs a result, the amount of chemical solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\n300 + 14t - 14t = 300\\,\\textrm{gal}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{300}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{lb}}{\\textrm{gal}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed chemical solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$, *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\\\\n&= (14\\frac{gal}{min}) \\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\frac{lb}{gal}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14Q}{300}\\frac{lb}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7Q}{150}\\frac{lb}{min}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 - \\frac{7Q}{150}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{7}{150}\\,Q\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} &= -\\frac{7}{150} \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt &= \\int(-\\frac{7}{150})dt \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot dQ &= -\\frac{7}{150}\\int1dt \\\\\n\\ln \\mid Q \\mid &= -\\frac{7t}{150} + C \\\\\n\\mid Q \\mid &= e^{-\\frac{7t}{150} + C} \\\\\nQ(t) &= Ae^{-7t/150}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 18$, and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(0) &= 18 \\\\\nAe^{-7 \\cdot 0/150} &= 18 \\\\\nA &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 18e^{-7t/150}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(25) = 18e^{-175/150} \\approx 5.61\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186114969 :question/problem "A $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$-capacity tank initially holds $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water containing $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s}$, while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}$. Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow."] [17592186114969 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t$. Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t$, in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}\n$$\n\nand $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}$. Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\textrm{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s}$. So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{in}^{′} &= (0\\frac{g}{mL}) \\cdot (100\\frac{mL}{s}) \\\\\n&= (0\\frac{g}{mL}) \\cdot (100\\frac{mL}{s}) \\\\\n&= 0\\frac{g}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t$.\n\nThe tank initially contains $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s}$, and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}$.\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nV(t) = 8\\,000 + 100t - 60t = 40(200+t)\\,\\textrm{mL}\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{40(200 + t)}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{mL}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}$, *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{out}^{′} &= (60\\frac{mL}{s}) \\cdot ((Q)/(40(200 + t))\\frac{g}{mL}) \\\\\n&= (60\\frac{mL}{s}) \\cdot ((Q)/(40(200 + t))\\frac{g}{mL}) \\\\\n&= (3Q)/(2(200 + t))\\frac{g}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dQ}{dt} &= Q_{in}^{′} - Q_{out}^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 - (3Q)/(2(200 + t))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{3}{2(200 + t)}\\,Q\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} &= - (3)/(2(200 + t)) \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt &= \\int(- (3)/(2(200 + t)))dt \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{Q} \\cdot dQ &= -\\frac{3}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{200 + t}dt \\\\\n\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}Q & &= -\\frac{3}{2}(\\ln \\mid 200 + t \\mid) + C \\\\ \\ln & Q\\end{vmatrix}|= \\ln \\mid (200 + t)^{-3/2} \\mid + C \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}Q & &= e^{\\ln \\mid (200 + t)^{-3/2}\\end{vmatrix}+ C} \\\\\nQ(t) &= (A)/((200 + t)^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 320$, and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(0) &= 320 \\\\\n(A)/((200 + 0)^{3/2}) &= 320 \\\\\nA &= 320(200)^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(t) &= (320(200)^{3/2})/((200 + t)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= 320(\\frac{200}{200 + t})^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$, so we can use the condition\n\n$$\nV(t_o) = 12\\,000\n$$\n\nto solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(t_{o}) &= 12000 \\\\\n40(200 + t_{o}) &= 12000 \\\\\n200 + t_{o} &= 300 \\\\\nt_{o} &= 100s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(100) &= 320(\\frac{200}{200 + 100})^{3/2} \\\\\n&= 320(\\frac{2}{3})^{3/2} \\\\\n&\\approx 174.19g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115798 :question/problem "A wastewater holding basin initially holds $450L$ of water containing $9kg$ of dissolved contaminants.\nClean water is pumped into the basin at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid leaves the basin at a rate of $25L/min$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminants in the basin $12$ minutes after the clean water pumping begins.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186115798 :question/answer-fields 17592186113695] [17592186115799 :question/problem "A $9000mL$-capacity tank initially holds $6000mL$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $180g$ of salt. Fresh water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90mL/s$, while the completely mixed brine flows out at a rate of $60mL/s$. Find the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186115799 :question/answer-fields 17592186113701] [17592186221985 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 4x - y - 8, y(1) = 2$\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n$y(2) \\approx - \\frac{1}{4}$\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(3)$.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$Δy = \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx$\n*where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186221985 :question/answer-fields 17592186220527] [17592186167142 :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = 2(x^{2} + y^{2})$ on the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin."] [17592186167142 :question/answer-fields 17592186163900] [17592186151806 :question/problem "Consider the following statement:\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $\\sqrt{2}$"] [17592186151806 :question/worked-solution "The sentence\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nThe sentence\n\n> $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg P \\lor \\neg Q\n$$"] [17592186151807 :question/problem "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |"] [17592186151807 :question/worked-solution "First, we add a column for\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nRemember that the truth values of\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nare opposite those of $Q$.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nThen, we add a column for\n\n$$\nP \\land \\neg Q\n$$\n\nRemember that\n\n$$\nP \\land \\neg Q\n$$\n\nis true if both $P$ and\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nare true.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\land \\neg Q\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for\n\n$$\n\\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\n$$\n\nRemember that the truth values of\n\n$$\n\\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\n$$\n\nare opposite those of\n\n$$\nP \\land \\neg Q\n$$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\land \\neg Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |"] [17592186151808 :question/problem "Simplify the statement $\\neg(\\neg C) \\lor \\neg(\\neg(C \\land D))$."] [17592186151808 :question/worked-solution "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So:\n\n- we can simplify $\\neg(\\neg C)$ to $C$, and\n- we can also simplify $\\neg(\\neg(C \\land D))$ to $C \\land D$.\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to\n\n$$\nC \\lor (C \\land D)\n$$"] [17592186151809 :question/problem "Consider the following statement.\n\n> $P: 1$ is **not** smaller than $2$\n\nWhich of following represents $\\neg P$?"] [17592186151809 :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"\n\n$$\n\\neg\n$$\n\n\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence.\n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n> It is **not** true that $1$ is **not** smaller than $2$.\n\nIn other words, $1$ is smaller than $2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nmeans the following:\n\n> $1$ is smaller than $2$.\n\nNote that $P$ is **false,** while\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nis **true.**"] [17592186152836 :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(¬A)∨¬B$."] [17592186152836 :question/answer-fields 17592186151493] [17592186147783 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} + y = xe^{x}$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_{1}(x) = \\sin x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\cos x$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$u_{1}\\sin x + u_{2}\\cos x$ {{field-1}}\nwhere $u_{1} = u_{1}(x), u_{2} = u_{2}(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n$u_{1}^{′}(\\sin x)^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(\\cos x)^{′}$ {{field-2}}"] [17592186147783 :question/answer-fields 17592186144378] [17592186147783 :question/answer-fields 17592186144383] [17592186129846 :question/answer-fields 17592186128527] [17592186129847 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y = 1 - x^{2}$ and the line $y =-3$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region"] [17592186129847 :question/answer-fields 17592186128533] [17592186128847 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=4$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R (y-z)\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2} \\:\\textrm{d}V$"] [17592186128847 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=(y-z)\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= f(ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ) \\\\\n&= (ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ - ρ\\cos ϕ) \\cdot ρ \\\\\n&= ρ^{2}(\\sin θ\\sin ϕ - \\cos ϕ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)(y - z)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV &= ∭_(Δ)ρ^{2}(\\sin θ\\sin ϕ - \\cos ϕ) \\cdot ρ^{2}\\sin ϕdρdθdϕ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}\\int_{0}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{2}ρ^{4}\\sin ϕ(\\sin θ\\sin ϕ - \\cos ϕ)dρdθdϕ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128848 :question/problem "![](images/95/95bb2c41fc54db270b8f6c884767868a8c691372a5d0910109f67582ef361ac6.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=10x$ for $y \\leq 0$ and $z \\leq 0$, shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$\\iiint\\limits_R \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}} \\:\\textrm{d}V$"] [17592186128848 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $5$ centered at $(5,0,0)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2=10x\n$$\n\nin spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} &= 10x \\\\\nρ^{2} &= 10ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ \\\\\nρ(ρ - 10\\cos θ\\sin ϕ) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have\n\n$$\n\\rho =10\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi\n$$\n\n![](images/53/53c68b2181ffbc7bd4a47206f67ee3da2af19d5f80683fd5ecb367c7d57bd47a.png)\n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 10\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\:\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= f(ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{ρ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}dV &= ∭_(Δ)\\frac{1}{ρ} \\cdot ρ^{2}\\sin ϕdρdθdϕ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/2}^{π}\\int_{3π/2}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{10\\cos θ\\sin ϕ}ρ\\sin ϕdρdθdϕ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128849 :question/problem "![](images/48/48a7c7ea2614c0b46df169cf353183f5b862f5feabab97a050107fe227f1521a.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 2z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} \\:\\textrm{d}V$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 9$ for $y \\leq 0$, shown above."] [17592186128849 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/cd/cd2f48915774902314c38f2b5e40baa646761ae14b560eab401d8841f61f5612.png)\n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 2z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= f(ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ) \\\\\n&= 2ρ\\cos ϕ \\cdot ρ \\\\\n&= 2ρ^{2}\\cos ϕ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)2z\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV &= ∭_(Δ)2ρ^{2}\\cos ϕ \\cdot ρ^{2}\\sin ϕdρdθdϕ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}\\int_{π}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{3}ρ^{4}\\sin 2ϕdρdθdϕ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin 2ϕdϕ \\cdot \\int_{π}^{2π}dθ \\cdot \\int_{0}^{3}ρ^{4}dρ \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2ϕ]_{ϕ = 0}^{ϕ = π} \\cdot [θ]_{θ = π}^{θ = 2π} \\cdot [\\frac{1}{5}ρ^{5}]_{ρ = 0}^{ρ = 3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(1 - 1) \\cdot (2π - π) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(243 - 0) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot π \\cdot \\frac{243}{5} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128865 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=4$ for $y \\leq 0$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2+z^2} \\:\\textrm{d}V$"] [17592186128865 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/46/4689af5de2bb93606060ff95ee2f2af1d34206c3ab89be8f854108c855a00aa3.png)\n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 2, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2+z^2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= f(ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{ρ^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV &= ∭_(Δ)\\frac{1}{ρ^{2}} \\cdot ρ^{2}\\sin ϕdρdθdϕ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}\\int_{π}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{2}\\sin ϕdρdθdϕ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129529 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 25$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)\\frac{xy}{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV$"] [17592186129529 :question/answer-fields 17592186126643] [17592186129755 :question/problem "![](images/33/3358dd177a848ea569fdeab6e39f4be268cc7325bb01c788e9bfe2ea2145d1eb.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 25$ for $y \\ge 0$, shown above."] [17592186129755 :question/answer-fields 17592186127981] [17592186129762 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 9$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)z\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV$"] [17592186129762 :question/answer-fields 17592186128023] [17592186129765 :question/problem "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4y$, shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)xdV$"] [17592186129765 :question/answer-fields 17592186128041] [17592186129766 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4$ for $x \\le 0$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)(x^{2} + y^{2})dV$"] [17592186129766 :question/answer-fields 17592186128047] [17592186119550 :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x)$, where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only."] [17592186119550 :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by\n\n$$\ny^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x)\n$$\n\nFirst, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(9C^{2}\\tan (x)) \\\\\n2yy^{′} &= 9C^{2}\\sec^{2} (x) \\\\\ny^{′} &= (9C^{2}\\sec^{2} (x))/(2y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C$. From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad C^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{9\\left(\\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}\\right)\\sec^2(x)}{2y} = \\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since we divide by $y$ when forming the orthogonal slope, the orthogonal-trajectory ODE is only valid where\n\n$$\ny\\neq 0\n$$\n\n(and where $\\tan(x)$ is defined). In particular, the original family forces $y=0$ whenever $\\tan(x)=0$."] [17592186119551 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}$, where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter."] [17592186119551 :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{C}{x^{2}}) \\\\\ny^{′} &= -\\frac{2C}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C$. From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{C}{x^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad C = x^2y\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{2C}{x^3} = -\\dfrac{2x^2y}{x^3} = -\\dfrac{2y}{x}\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{2y}{x}\\right)} = \\dfrac{x}{2y}\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{x}{2y}\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= x \\\\\n\\int2y \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\intxdx \\\\\n\\int2ydy &= \\intxdx \\\\\ny^{2} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + K \\\\\n2y^{2} - x^{2} &= K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n2y^2 - x^2 = K\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/82/82bd1506c86aff662ba47618c0a6cdaf8ed1db3fa617be301fcb2d318373a913.png)\n\n**Note**: Since\n\n$$\nC\\neq0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\ny\\neq0\n$$\n\non every curve in the family, so slopes $y'$ and $'_\\perp$ are well-defined."] [17592186120271 :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy = C^{2}$, where $C \\ne 0$ is a parameter."] [17592186120271 :question/answer-fields 17592186117735] [17592186120273 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $x^{2} = y^{2} + C$, where $C$ and $K$ are parameters."] [17592186120273 :question/answer-fields 17592186117747] [17592186157621 :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n$x = u^{2} + v^{2}, y =-v$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_{2}$ under the action of $T$?"] [17592186157621 :question/answer-fields 17592186153940] [17592186157686 :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n$x = u(v + 1), y = 4u(v - u)$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_{3}$ under the action of $T$?"] [17592186157686 :question/answer-fields 17592186154573]]